<p>Kneusel uses NumPy’s <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.indexing.html#integer-array-indexing">integer array indexing</a> to randomize <code>y</code> and the rows of <code>x</code> using the same rearrangement in each. Here’s what he does:</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; idx = np.argsort(np.random.random(x.shape[0]))
&gt;&gt;&gt; x = x[idx, :]
&gt;&gt;&gt; y = y[idx]
</code></pre>
<p>The first line starts by generating an array of 150 (<code>x.shape[0]</code> is 150) random numbers between 0 and 1 via <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/generated/numpy.random.random.html"><code>random.random</code></a>; <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/2.5/reference/generated/numpy.argsort.html"><code>argsort</code></a> then returns an array of the indices of the sorted version of that array. So <code>idx</code> is an array of the integers from 0 through 149 in random order. The two lines after that rearrange <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> in that same random order.</p>
<p>I don’t know about you, but I see this as a weird way to go about randomization. I realize you can’t just shuffle <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> individually, as that would break the connection between an iris’s measurements and its class. But why go through two steps when you can create the <code>idx</code> array directly?</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; idx = np.random.permutation(x.shape[0])
</code></pre>
<p>Maybe Kneusel’s method is faster, even though it seems like it should be slower. This won’t matter for the small iris dataset, but he may be preparing his readers for huge datasets to come.</p>
<p>I tested the two methods using <a href="https://ipython.readthedocs.io/en/stable/interactive/magics.html#magic-timeit">IPython’s <code>%timeit</code> magic command</a>. Here’s what I found, with 1,000,000 indices instead of 150:</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; %timeit np.argsort(np.random.random(1_000_000))
63.8 ms ± 434 μs per loop (mean ± std. dev. of 7 runs, 10 loops each)

&gt;&gt;&gt; %timeit np.random.permutation(1_000_000)
5.98 ms ± 17.8 μs per loop (mean ± std. dev. of 7 runs, 100 loops each)
</code></pre>
<p>So the simple code is both more direct <em>and</em> about an order of magnitude faster. I’ll be looking at the code in the book with a more critical eye as I proceed—assuming I don’t bail out and look for another machine learning reference.</p>
<hr/>
<p>You may have noticed that the code above uses NumPy’s older random number system. The current approach would be to create a <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/generator.html#permutations"><code>Generator</code></a> instance and use that as the basis for later function calls. Would this have any effect on the timing results? Let’s see.</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; rng = np.random.default_rng()
&gt;&gt;&gt; %timeit np.argsort(rng.random(1_000_000))
65 ms ± 188 μs per loop (mean ± std. dev. of 7 runs, 10 loops each)

&gt;&gt;&gt; %timeit rng.permutation(1_000_000)
4.91 ms ± 10.9 μs per loop (mean ± std. dev. of 7 runs, 100 loops each)
</code></pre>
<p>No, the runtimes are about the same.</p>
